NNAAMMEE
       ppmwheel - make a PPM image of a color wheel


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppppmmwwhheeeell _d_i_a_m_e_t_e_r [{--hhuueevvaalluuee | --hhuueessaattuurraattiioonn}] [--mmaaxxvvaall==_N]



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmwwhheeeell  produces a PPM image of a color wheel of the specified diame‐
       ter inside a white square just large enough to hold it.

       The color wheel is based on the HSV color model.  Hues are distributed
         angularly around the circle and the values and saturations  are  dis‐
       tributed
         radially.

       You can generate three kinds of color wheel:


       •      Hue-value: Select this with a --hhuueevvaalluuee option.

              The  saturation is 100% everywhere.  The value goes from zero to
              100%
                linearly, from the center of the wheel to the  edge.   So  the
              center is black.

              Hence,  the  image consists of all of the secondary colors based
              on the
                red, green, and blue primary colors.  A secondary color is one
              that is
                composed of light of at most two of the three primary colors.


       •      Hue-saturation: Select this with a --hhuueessaattuurraattiioonn option.

              The  value is 100% everywhere.  The saturation goes from zero to
              100%
                linearly, from the center of the wheel to the  edge.   So  the
              center is white.


       •      Ppmcirc:  Select this by not specifying any other wheel type op‐
              tion.

              The saturation is 100% everywhere.  The value is a strange func‐
              tion of
                the distance from the center, increasing as the square root of
              the distance
                until halfway out, then decreasing as the 8th root of the dis‐
              tance the rest
                of  the  way.  We don't know what the point of this is, but it
              is what the
                program Ppmcirc by Peter Kirchgessner in 1995  does,  and  was
              the only option
                in ppppmmwwhheeeell from its inception in 2003 to 2019.





AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
       You must specify one non-option argument: the radius of the color wheel
       in pixels.

       This is also the height and width of the output image.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppppmmwwhheeeell recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --hhuueevvaalluuee

              This selects a hue-value color wheel.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).


       --hhuueessaattuurraattiioonn

              This selects a hue-saturation color wheel.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).


       --mmaaxxvvaall==_N

              This selects the maxval for the image.  The default is 255.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).





